// iteration-G11, Parte P/Q: safety cap so a real-hardware run can't spin
// unbounded if the guest just keeps cycling grBufferClear/grBufferSwap
// with no new, not-yet-implemented Glide call ever appearing in between
// (both are real implementations now, so the generic trap alone would
// never catch this case). After this many COMPLETED swaps with no new
// call observed, grBufferSwap_impl halts cleanly on its own and reports
// FRAME_LOOP_NO_NEW_GLIDE_CALL, per this iteration's explicit instruction
// not to let it "run infinitely."
// iteration-G14, Parte A (SUPERSEDED by G15, see below): first raised from
// G11's original 4 to a 120-cycle ceiling with an 8-second wall-clock
// cap. G14's real-hardware run falsified the 8-second assumption: only 3
// presents happened in ~18 real seconds (each frame doing substantial
// legitimate guest work -- asset loads -- between presents), so the time
// cap tripped after only ~1-2 presents, far too early.
