// NEW THIS ITERATION: identified the real allocator-caller function at
// 0x0044FE70 (previously only its tail, 0x0044fec1-0x0044ff07, was
// disassembled in G39/G40) -- its full body is a texture PALETTE-REMAP
// routine: a 64KB loop (0x0044fe8d-0x0044fea8) that walks a source
// index buffer at ds:0x004e25c0, translates each byte through a 256-
// entry table at ds:0x0061e020, writes the result to ds:0x004e25bf,
// THEN calls guTexAllocateMemory+guTexDownloadMipMap (the tail G39/G40
// already covered) and returns the handle in EAX. It has exactly TWO
// static call sites in the whole binary (0x00409c5f, 0x00415887) --
// i.e. only two distinct places in Ignition ever prepare+allocate a
// texture this way. This narrows "which asset is allocation #24" to a
// tractable future search (walk forward from those two call sites'
// enclosing functions), not yet completed this iteration.
//
// REAL GrMipMapId_t NUMERIC RANGE (Part 12-14/24, directly audited):
// real Glide 2.x's reference guTexAllocateMemory does NOT return a
// small sequential index (0,1,2,3,...) -- consistent with every real
// Glide/Voodoo reference this project has cited, a GrMipMapId_t is a
// BYTE OFFSET into the allocated TMU (texture memory unit) address
// space, monotonically advancing by however many bytes each texture
// occupies. A single 256x256 8bpp texture alone is 65536 bytes -- so on
// REAL Voodoo hardware, GrMipMapId_t values exceed 0x100 (256) after
// the very first non-trivial allocation, not because of any bug, but by
// design. This is an important negative result: it means Part 25's
// "redesign to small SDK-compatible IDs" would NOT reliably keep this
// project's handles under 0x100 either, even done with maximal fidelity
// to the real contract -- a "small ID" scheme would only coincidentally
// satisfy [esi+0x18]'s bounds check for the first texture or two, then
// fail again exactly the same way for the third/fourth/... allocation.
// This is strong evidence AGAINST "our arbitrary 0x70000001+ numbering
// broke an implicit small-ID assumption" (Part 13) as the general fix:
// no numeric handle scheme -- ours or a faithfully-real one -- keeps
// GrMipMapId_t values under 256 once more than one or two textures
// exist, and this run is well past allocation #24. Masking to 8 bits
// (Part 22-23, already independently forbidden) would not even be
// principled here, since a real handle's low byte carries no reserved
// meaning either.
//
// CONCLUSION (Success Case C continues to hold, refined): the exact
// numeric coincidence (0x70000018 == handle #24) is compelling but, per
// the above, the mechanism is NOT "opaque handles are numerically too
// large by design" -- any faithful handle would ALSO have exceeded
// 0x100 by allocation #24. The remaining live explanations are (a) a
// genuine data-flow bug in Ignition's OWN code -- some instruction
// copies a texture handle value into this particle's index field,
// whether by index/table confusion, a stale/reused struct slot, or a
// union of "textured particle" and "flat-colored particle" variants of
// the same record -- that would very likely ALSO manifest on real
// Voodoo hardware once enough textures were allocated (this project's
// specific handle VALUES don't need to match real ones for this to
// reproduce -- only the ORDER/COUNT of allocations before this particle
// renders matters); or (b) a coincidence of this run's particular
// timing/memory reuse that a real hardware run's different allocation
// count/order would not hit. Distinguishing these needs to see whether
// [0x01039DD8] was ALREADY corrupted the first time this project ever
// observes it, or transitions mid-run from a plausible small value --
// exactly what the new [G53-WATCH] one-address value probe (win32/
